okay  the sound of the explosion  the calls for help  the panic and confusion what should have  followed was in the words of the  chairman of the public inquiry an  effective coordinated multi-agency  response that he said is not what  happened  we heard heartbreaking evidence  of the injured and The Rescuers who were  in the city room  hearing the Sirens of ambulances  knowing paramedics were close by  expecting their imminent arrival  only for them not to arrive those delays  prove fatal to John Atkinson his family  now know he would probably have survived  with proper treatment as we know from  Witnesses  John kept asking  causes us to say that is too great to  put into words  with the family of the youngest victim  eight-year-old sathy Rose russos filmed  here on that night disagreed with the  finding that earlier treatment wouldn't  have saved her  she died through the loss of blood  and her injuries of course but a cause  of death was loss of blood  now if you leave a person that's  bleeding and don't give them the  immediate  medical attention that they require then  of course they're going to die she's a  fighter like a mum she would fight to  the end and again for the family of  Martin head the hope is that the  inquiry's recommendations will make a  difference I don't want any of them to  have died for nothing  what's really reassuring is that Sir  John is actually going to follow up some  of these things to make sure they are  actually carried out and that is  reassuring  the Emergency Services came to repeat  their apologies for a catalog of  mistakes they say changes have been made  if this were to happen again tomorrow  how do you reassure the public that some  of these mistakes all of these mistakes  wouldn't be made again God forbid we  should have to do this again  that some of the things that went wrong  almost as a domino from not getting it  right at the outset would just not  happen again even though the Threat  Level was very high in May 2017 the  chairman said no one in Authority really  thought it could happen to them they  were wrong and five years on so many are  still paying the price Greg Milam Sky  News Manchester

